WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.140
Its names are many, from the Golden Perfect
to the Lasso of Hestia.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.800
But its purpose is singular — it compels
those within its bonds to tell the truth.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.030
Where did this fabulous rope come from?

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:15.120
Well, we're here to divine the truth of Wonder
Woman’s lasso.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:21.550
The lasso began as part of the goddess Aphrodite's
magical girdle, an item bestowed upon the

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:24.840
Amazons that granted them strength and immortality.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:30.480
Queen Hyppolita, intrigued by its properties,
instructed her craftswoman, Metala, to forge

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.099
a golden rope from it.

00:00:32.099 --> 00:00:37.590
Metala did so, creating the very first iteration
of Wonder Woman's wonderful lasso.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:42.200
It was a finely-wrought golden chain that
didn't, in fact, compel its subject to tell

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.060
the truth but to obey.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:50.090
It was unbreakable, magically elastic, capable
of binding groups of people, and, once coated

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:55.600
in chemicals of Amazonian design, was able
to transform Diana's civilian clothes into

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.650
her Wonder Woman costume.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:02.630
Then DC's landmark event, Crisis on Infinite
Earths, took place.

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:08.050
Wonder Woman's lasso was left relatively unchanged,
save for two major alterations.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:13.750
It now compelled the truth rather than obedience,
and it was no longer an Amazonian design.

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:19.320
This post-Crisis lasso was instead forged
by Hephaestus, god of metallurgy himself,

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:23.480
from the Golden Girdle of Gaea, tempered in
the fires of Hestia.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:28.840
But that wasn't the end of the lasso's changes,
as the New 52 reboot of the DC universe changed

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.250
the legendary tool still further.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:36.790
Nowadays, the lasso is known to the Amazons
as "the Golden Perfect," a gift from the gods

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:38.930
passed down through the generations.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:43.640
It's a symbol of sacred trust — that of
the Amazons in the gods and the gods in the

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:44.640
Amazons.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.060
Only an Amazon of purest heart can wield it,
making Diana a perfect candidate.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.970
Generally speaking, the lasso's powers seem
fairly straightforward.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:58.330
Wonder Woman wraps a bad guy in it, bad guy
has to tell the truth about his evil scheme,

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:01.549
and the heroes rush off to stop it with their
newfound knowledge.

00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:06.390
But truth is a slippery slope, and Wonder
Woman's deeply held sense of compassion will

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:10.690
never allow her to settle for convenience
when there's honesty to be had.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:14.410
Luckily, the lasso's powers are flexible enough
to account for this.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:19.660
Under the umbrella of "truth," the lasso can
do everything from dispelling illusions induced

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:24.849
by hallucinatory gas to allowing people to
communicate openly beyond a stubborn language

00:02:24.849 --> 00:02:25.900
barrier.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:30.560
In the recent "Year One" storyline, a neophyte
Wonder Woman, dazzled by the delights and

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:35.640
depredations of Man's World, used the lasso,
then still new to her, to accomplish both

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.050
of these feats.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:43.320
When she, Etta Candy, Steve Trevor, and Dr.
Barbara Ann Minerva all held the lasso at

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:48.500
once, they were able to communicate despite
Diana's inability to then speak English.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:54.820
Soon after, an insanity-inducing poison turned
a squad of helpless men into manic murderers.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:59.489
With the lasso, Diana was able to dispel the
poison's effects immediately and discern the

00:02:59.489 --> 00:03:01.120
men's innocence.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:05.440
Truth might need to be delivered as a magical
antidote or immersive, immediate language

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:08.250
course, but the lasso will always be able
to fit the bill.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:14.000
Despite its amazing powers, Wonder Woman's
lasso is, in fact, a simple lasso.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:19.670
Sure, it's an unbreakable one that can stretch,
shrink, and form complicated nets and snares,

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:21.680
but it's a rope with a loop tied into it.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.349
Wonder Woman uses it often in this manner,
throwing it to catch midair villains and rampaging

00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:27.349
animals alike.

00:03:27.349 --> 00:03:32.379
Or when a disaster comes along, the lasso
is useful for towing unlucky victims to high

00:03:32.379 --> 00:03:35.760
ground, the shore, or beyond a fire's reach.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.830
In the Golden and Silver Ages, however, Wonder
Woman explored the lasso's physical properties

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.550
to the furthest extent.

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:47.349
As the character wasn't originally capable
of flight, she could twirl the lasso furiously

00:03:47.349 --> 00:03:50.180
enough to create air currents she could float
on.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:54.209
Wonder Woman was practically a cowgirl during
this era, so skillfully did she wield her

00:03:54.209 --> 00:03:55.819
magical chain.

00:03:55.819 --> 00:04:00.500
Gal Gadot might be able to unleash devastating
shockwaves from her bracelets, but we'd like

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:05.629
to see her give someone a serious earache
with a particularly fierce twirl of the lasso.

00:04:06.629 --> 00:04:09.970
Confronting foes with upsetting truths has
its uses.

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:14.519
More than once, Diana has used the lasso's
powers to force those within its bonds to

00:04:14.519 --> 00:04:20.380
face a secret that motivates them to do ill
or to face consequences they'd prefer to ignore.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:25.870
In 2008's Wonder Woman #15, Diana confronted
Captain Nazi, an old foe looking to bring

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:28.030
the Third Reich back to life.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:33.210
Seeking to learn of his plan to invade Themyscira,
Diana wrapped him in the lasso and discovered

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:34.650
his past, as well.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:39.240
Facing his abusive childhood brought him to
tears, which had the dual effect of stopping

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.750
his assault and forcing him to lose faith
in his campaign.

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:48.050
Similarly, during George Pérez's run on Wonder
Woman, Diana stopped an emboldened Ares at

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:50.560
the point when all seemed lost.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:55.400
In the lasso's coils, he saw the result of
his quest to cover the Earth in conflict and

00:04:55.400 --> 00:05:01.080
bloodshed: a barren rock, void of followers,
and thus void of the discord that fueled him.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:05.699
The abject loneliness of this vision didn't
just compel him to retreat, but it actually

00:05:05.699 --> 00:05:07.669
wrenched tears from his eyes.

00:05:07.669 --> 00:05:11.669
In the end, it wasn't violence that stopped
either of these terrible villains, but the

00:05:11.669 --> 00:05:14.060
truth that was lurking within their own hearts.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:20.070
Unfortunately, not all who seek Wonder Woman's
destruction can be constrained by the lasso.

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:25.380
Genocide, a villain envisioned by Wonder Woman
writer Gail Simone as being the heroine's

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:30.479
ultimate antithesis, actually used the lasso
to further her own depraved aims.

00:05:30.479 --> 00:05:35.160
A superpowered juggernaut created from both
Diana's corpse — retrieved from the future

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:41.160
— and soil from the sites of 20th-century
killing fields, Genocide was a ruthless golem

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:46.669
of murderous intent, used to further the aims
of Ares, the Cheetah, and other members of

00:05:46.669 --> 00:05:48.680
Wonder Woman's rogues gallery.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:54.290
In her first assault upon the Amazon, Genocide
was actually able to steal the lasso and take

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:59.520
it back to her terrible masters, who surgically
implanted it within Genocide's body.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:05.259
Twisted into serving horrible aims, the lasso
imbued Genocide with incredible power.

00:06:05.259 --> 00:06:10.080
Suddenly, she could overthrow the will of
Green Lantern Corps members, manipulate those

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:16.460
around her into disabling despair, and exude
a general aura of rage and hopelessness.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:21.820
All reason and rationality could be overthrown
in one terrible flood of psychic energy from

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:25.600
the lasso that Genocide had buried in her
own flesh.

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.410
Though Diana was able to retrieve it and Genocide
was scattered to the winds, the memory of

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:34.860
what the lasso was capable of when put to
evil ends was not so easily forgotten.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:41.830
Being an object and not a power she herself
is imbued with, Wonder Woman can use the lasso

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:43.160
upon herself.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:47.330
Rarely does she need to employ this ability
— she makes a point of being honest with

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:51.699
herself and others from the get go — but
it's come in handy more often than you might

00:06:51.699 --> 00:06:52.699
think.

00:06:52.699 --> 00:06:58.600
Usually, this comes in the form of Diana wrapping
herself in it to prove she speaks truthfully.

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:02.840
Sometimes it's a private practice meant to
reconnect the heroine to her roots and her

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:03.840
mission.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:08.270
But occasionally, she needs the lasso in a
moment of extreme crisis, and those are often

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:10.610
the most spectacular uses of all.

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:16.630
During 2016's "Year One" storyline, Wonder
Woman was exposed to a fear and rage-inducing

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:17.630
gas.

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:22.350
Plunged into a mindless fury, she promised
to anoint the men of this new world with the

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.250
blood they seemed crave.

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:28.630
But clarity managed to shine through, and
she managed to grab hold of her lasso and

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:32.010
wrap it around her own body, head to toe,
as tightly as possible.

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:37.050
Her mind cleared of anger and despair, and
she dispersed her own insanity in a dazzling

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:39.250
blaze of power, crying out:

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:41.070
"This is not true!"

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:46.330
The day was saved, the villains were caught,
and Wonder Woman, fresh from Themyscira's

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:51.069
shores, had discovered just how much of a
difference she and her wonderful lasso could

00:07:51.069 --> 00:07:52.610
make in this strange new world.

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:58.440
Just as Wonder Woman has her lasso, the girls
who've taken on the mantle of Wonder Girl

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:00.080
must have lassos of their own.

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:01.870
But they don't have lassos of truth.

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:06.830
Instead, they have lassos of very different
powers, suited for very different heroines.

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:12.830
Donna Troy, a Wonder Girl of many divergent
origin stories, wields a silver-blue lasso

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:16.550
with as much dexterity as the heroine she
takes her name from.

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:22.660
In 2010's Justice League of America #44, she
named it as the Lasso of Persuasion.

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:25.430
As she told the demon Etrigan, bound within
it:

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:29.890
"Anyone whose will is less than [hers] has
to do what [she] tells them.

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:31.500
And [her] will is iron!"

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:36.649
Cassie Sandsmark, the second Wonder Girl,
wields a very different lasso, the Lasso of

00:08:36.649 --> 00:08:37.649
Lightning.

00:08:37.649 --> 00:08:42.269
This lasso is uniquely connected to her as
a daughter of Zeus, channeling her godly father's

00:08:42.269 --> 00:08:45.389
legendary lightning through its unbreakable
bonds.

00:08:45.389 --> 00:08:50.379
Moreover, as was revealed when the witch Circe
had the Lasso of Lightning under her control

00:08:50.379 --> 00:08:55.560
in 2006's Wonder Woman #4, it's also capable
of inducing rage.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:09:00.529
Whether its truth, persuasion, or lightning,
the lassos of the DC universe are not to be

00:09:00.529 --> 00:09:01.529
trifled with.

00:09:02.529 --> 00:09:06.379
If there's one thing you know about Wonder
Woman's creator, William Moulton Marston,

00:09:06.379 --> 00:09:08.759
it's probably that he invented the lie detector.

00:09:08.759 --> 00:09:12.999
But this nugget of common knowledge isn't
precisely true.

00:09:12.999 --> 00:09:17.360
Marston created the systolic blood pressure
test, not the polygraph itself, and he never

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.189
accomplished his goal of introducing it to
the court system.

00:09:20.189 --> 00:09:25.809
He still managed to make good on his contribution,
appeared in a series of Gillette advertisements,

00:09:25.809 --> 00:09:29.820
and he got many of his ideas to the public
through the comics he'd become best known

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:30.820
for.

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:34.410
But the common understanding of Marston's
invention having something to do with Wonder

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:40.100
Woman's Lasso of Truth is solidly wrong, especially
since his rendition of the lasso compelled

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:42.260
obedience instead of truth-telling.

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:46.840
In fact, Marston saw Wonder Woman's lasso
as symbolizing something very different: The

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:51.990
natural allure he believed all women possessed,
that the world would do well to heed.

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:56.450
As detailed in Jill Lepore's landmark book
The Secret History of Wonder Woman, Marston

00:09:56.450 --> 00:10:01.820
saw the Amazons as ruling through natural
charm, grace, wisdom, and strength.

00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:05.829
The lasso merely got people to accept them
as rightful leaders a little more quickly

00:10:05.829 --> 00:10:07.379
than they would have otherwise.

00:10:07.379 --> 00:10:09.809
"Marston's really endlessly fascinating.

00:10:09.809 --> 00:10:15.150
There's kind of almost not an effort in American
culture that he doesn't have a hand in in

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:17.649
the first half of the 21st century."

00:10:17.649 --> 00:10:23.379
Truth was important, sure, but accepting Amazonian
guidance was the real path to a just and peaceful

00:10:23.379 --> 00:10:24.379
world.

00:10:24.379 --> 00:10:26.579
That's a little harder to turn into a soundbite
than:

00:10:26.579 --> 00:10:30.319
"[The] man who invented the lie detector also
invented the Lasso of Truth."

00:10:31.319 --> 00:10:34.399
The Lasso of Truth is, crucially, unbreakable.

00:10:34.399 --> 00:10:38.899
What good would it be otherwise, in a world
of razor-sharp batarangs and beings of godlike

00:10:38.899 --> 00:10:39.899
strength?

00:10:39.899 --> 00:10:43.949
Wonder Woman can't go around having it snapped
by any old bad guy in a super-powered exo-skeleton.

00:10:43.949 --> 00:10:48.679
No, as befitting a tool forged by the gods,
the lasso is unbreakable.

00:10:49.679 --> 00:10:53.989
Though it's always climactic and heralds an
enormous change of stakes, the lasso has,

00:10:53.989 --> 00:10:56.129
in fact, been broken before.

00:10:56.129 --> 00:11:01.059
Sometimes it's the result of having its connection
to truth weakened on the conceptual level.

00:11:01.059 --> 00:11:07.379
Bizarro, representing the reversal of all
things, was able to break it in 2003's Batman/Superman/Wonder

00:11:07.379 --> 00:11:09.480
Woman: Trinity series.

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:15.399
But in 2003's Superman: Red Son, Wonder Woman
broke it herself in a desperate attempt to

00:11:15.399 --> 00:11:16.730
save Superman.

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:20.730
Occasionally, it's broken through Diana's
own weakness.

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:26.850
For example, her dismay at learning a truth
she couldn't handle in 1997's JLA #62 ripped

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:30.499
the lasso apart through the sheer force of
the quandary at hand.

00:11:30.499 --> 00:11:32.850
Naturally, it's always returned to wholeness.

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:39.089
But the lasso, as its mistress, is not invulnerable,
which provides readers with a wealth of stories.

00:11:39.089 --> 00:11:41.319
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:11:41.319 --> 00:11:45.139
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
superheroes are coming soon.

00:11:45.139 --> 00:11:47.389
